    "result_text": "The build succeeds and the test passes (there's only one test class; the other files are base/support classes).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented membership level 4 gated on tenure, keeping each piece of logic in its natural home.\n\n**Where the logic went:**\n\n1. **`Owner.getTenureDays()`** (domain model) \u2014 tenure is a property of the owner, so I added a small `@Transient` accessor that computes whole days from `registrationDate` to today, mirroring the existing `getAgeBand()` pattern. A newly created owner (registered today) has zero tenure.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.resolveMembershipLevel(...)`** (level-scoring rule) \u2014 extended the existing additive formula with a fourth factor: `+1` when `getTenureDays() > 365`, guarded by a named constant `TENURE_LEVEL_THRESHOLD_DAYS = 365`. Since the pre-tenure factors (base `1` + email + no-namesake) cap at `3`, the tenure point is the only way to reach `4` \u2014 so **level 4 requires tenure of more than 365 days** falls out as a necessary condition. I removed the now-obsolete `Math.min(level, 3)` cap.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipLevel` schema description and raised `maximum` from `3` to `4` to match the new behavior.\n\n**Verification:** A new owner with an email and `namesakeCount` 0 scores `1 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 3` (zero tenure earns no fourth point), so `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` passes \u2014 a new owner never exceeds level 3, exactly as specified. `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
